WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.457
A bright factory unfolds from paper
shapes.

00:00:03.457 --> 00:00:09.070
Each new product carries a revenue token,
but a second stream of cost tokens

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:10.272
follows close behind.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:21.223
At first, revenue grows faster than cost.
The space between the two streams

00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:25.939
widens, suggesting that a small increase
in output can improve profit.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.907
More products require a lower selling
price and a heavier workload.

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:39.552
The profit strip still grows, but each new
addition becomes smaller than

00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:39.939
before.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.702
A pair of moving bars approaches equal
height.

00:00:48.702 --> 00:00:52.518
They represent marginal revenue and
marginal cost, creating an interior

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:55.189
candidate where the profit slope becomes
zero.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.534
A capacity gate cuts across the production
line.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:08.220
Even a promising extra unit may be
impossible, so the best allowed decision

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.022
can sit at that boundary.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.694
The factory waits with its curves and
limits visible.

00:01:18.694 --> 00:01:22.415
Should it make one more unit, stop here,
or move backward?

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.106
Give a decision with evidence.
